# =============================================================================
# ReserveToday — Backend env
# =============================================================================
# Required keys for v0 (admin portal) are flagged [required].
# Deferred keys can be left blank until the corresponding slice ships.

# ---- App ---------------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_ENV=development
# Deployment environment NAME (development | staging | production). Separate from
# NODE_ENV, which stays "production" on any server. Drives the Sentry environment
# tag and whether Sentry reports at all (off when development).
APP_ENV=development
PORT=4000
# Background cron jobs (src/jobs/index.ts) — PT request expiry + credit auto-refund,
# package expiry, waiver flagging. Off means members' credits are never returned.
ENABLE_JOBS=true

# ---- Database [required] -----------------------------------------------------
# Compose docker postgres locally — these four feed both docker-compose and
# the connection string, which is assembled in src/db/url.ts.
POSTGRES_USER=postgres
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres
POSTGRES_DB=reservetoday
POSTGRES_PORT=5433
# Password for the `booking_app` role — the NON-owner, non-superuser role the
# server connects as. It matters because Postgres lets superusers and table
# owners bypass Row-Level Security: connect as POSTGRES_USER above and the
# tenant policies (migration 0033) stop enforcing anything, silently.
#
# `npm run db:migrate` creates the role with exactly this password, and
# src/db/url.ts builds DATABASE_APP_URL from it, so the two cannot drift. Local
# dev can leave it blank (a dev-only default is used); on a server it is the
# DB_APP_PASSWORD environment secret and is required — unset, the migrate step
# refuses rather than provisioning a role nothing knows the password to.
DB_APP_PASSWORD=
# The two connection strings, both assembled from the parts above and both
# overridable. DATABASE_URL is the owner (migrations + seeds); DATABASE_APP_URL
# is the `booking_app` role the server runs as. Deployments set both explicitly.
# DATABASE_URL=
# DATABASE_APP_URL=

# ---- Superadmin bootstrap [required] -----------------------------------------
# Passwordless bootstrap. The seed (db/seed/superadmin.ts) creates a staff-pool
# Better Auth user with this email — no password is stored anywhere. On first
# sign-in the operator clicks "Forgot password" and is mailed a link to set their
# own password. Re-running the seed never touches an existing user's credential
# (so subsequent deploys don't lock them out).
SUPERADMIN_EMAIL=

# ---- Super portal [optional] -------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated addresses allowed to operate the super portal at
# admin.portal.<root domain> — creating, listing and suspending studios via
# /api/v1/platform/*.
#
# This is NOT a staff role. `staff_users.role = 'superadmin'` says what someone
# may do inside one studio; a studio's own superadmin must not be able to
# suspend another studio, so platform administration lives outside the database
# entirely. This is the WHOLE allowlist — SUPERADMIN_EMAIL above is deliberately
# not folded in, because doing so handed the first studio's superadmin the run of
# every other studio. Leaving this blank means a super portal nobody can reach,
# which the backend warns about at boot.
PLATFORM_ADMIN_EMAILS=askblueprintagency@gmail.com

# ---- Origins [required] ------------------------------------------------------
# The WHOLE allowlist: CORS, the auth pools' trusted origins, the public-route `Origin`
# validation — and, read backwards, the base of every link the backend mails a
# studio's staff or members and every URL Stripe redirects them back to.
#
# Comma-separated, with the wildcard as the LEFTMOST label — it stands for the
# tenant slug and covers exactly one label. Creating a tenant is a row insert,
# so its origin cannot be listed in advance; this is what makes all of the above
# work for a studio that did not exist when the backend was deployed.
#   local:      http://*.localhost:3000,http://*.portal.localhost:3001
#   staging:    https://*.dev.reservetoday.app,https://*.portal.dev.reservetoday.app
#   production: https://*.reservetoday.app,https://*.portal.reservetoday.app
#
# An exact origin is accepted too, for a host that names no tenant — the bare
# `http://localhost:3000` below is one, and requests from it fall back to tenant
# #1. That is the only reason to add one: this replaced PORTAL_ORIGIN and
# CLIENT_ORIGIN, whose single values named one studio's two apps and put that
# studio's identity into every other studio's emails.
#
# ORDER MATTERS for the *wildcards*. This list is read backwards to build the
# links the backend mails and hands Stripe, and the FIRST wildcard for each app
# wins. Keep the two real tenant wildcards first. A second wildcard added for
# some other purpose — a preview domain like `https://*.vercel.app`, say —
# listed ahead of them would silently become the base of every studio's emails.
# Exact origins are safe anywhere in the list; only wildcards are ever chosen
# as a link base. The backend refuses to boot if the list yields no wildcard
# for one of the two apps.
TENANT_ORIGIN_PATTERNS=http://*.localhost:3000,http://*.portal.localhost:3001,http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:3001

# ---- Better Auth [required] --------------------------------------------------
# Self-hosted auth: three user pools (client = members, staff = studio portals,
# platform = the super portal), each on its own tables and base path. Sessions
# are bearer tokens. See docs/adr/0004-self-hosted-auth-with-better-auth.md.
#
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET signs every session token and encrypts the stored 2FA
# secrets, for all three pools (client, staff, platform). At least 32 chars.
# Rotating it signs everyone out and invalidates enrolled authenticator apps.
# Generate locally with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
# The backend's own public origin — the base of every link Better Auth mails
# (password resets). The auth routes live under /api/v1/auth/{client,staff,platform}.
#   local: http://localhost:4000   staging: https://api.dev.reservetoday.app
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:4000

# ---- Impersonation grant signing [required] ----------------------------------
# HS256 secret for the BE-signed JWT that proves a /api/v1/me/* call is being
# made by a superadmin impersonating a client. Must be at least 32 chars.
# Generate locally with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
IMPERSONATION_SECRET=

# ---- Mail (Resend) [required] -----------------------------------------------
# Outbound mail goes through Resend's HTTP API (be/src/lib/mailer.ts). Create
# the key at resend.com → API Keys, "Sending access", restricted to the
# platform's verified domain.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# The platform's two envelope addresses, both in the domain verified on Resend.
# A member's mail leaves on MAIL_FROM_EMAIL; a staff member's (admin +
# instructor) on MAIL_FROM_PORTAL_EMAIL, which falls back to the first when
# blank. The studio's own name and Reply-To are per-tenant data, not env: see
# docs/md/mail-identity.md.
MAIL_FROM_EMAIL=
MAIL_FROM_PORTAL_EMAIL=
MAIL_FROM_NAME=ReserveToday

# =============================================================================
# DEFERRED — fill when the corresponding slice lands
# =============================================================================

# ---- Stripe (deferred — payments slice) --------------------------------------
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Must match the statement descriptor prefix set on the Stripe account. The
# studio's name is appended per charge in what's left of the 22-character limit;
# leave blank and every studio charges under the platform's name alone.
STRIPE_STATEMENT_DESCRIPTOR_PREFIX=

# ---- Cloudflare R2 -----------------------------------------------------------
# S3-compatible object storage. ONE bucket holds everything: instructor and
# workshop imagery AND leave Supporting Documents. It is served by R2_PUBLIC_URL, so
# every object in it is readable by anyone holding the key.
# Leave R2_BUCKET_NAME blank and document upload reports itself unavailable
# (boot is fine).
#
# R2_PUBLIC_URL is the CDN hostname, never the bucket's own pub-<hash>.r2.dev
# URL — Cloudflare rate-limits that one and does not intend it for production.
# It is https://cdn.reservetoday.app in BOTH deployed environments, served by the
# `cdn/` Vercel project, which keeps r2.dev as a private origin.
#
# Staging and production deliberately share one bucket behind that one host, so a
# staging upload is visible on production URLs and a staging delete removes it
# from production. See cdn/README.md.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET_NAME=
R2_PUBLIC_URL=

# ---- Tests (local only) ------------------------------------------------------
# Scratch Postgres database for the integration tests. Deliberately NOT in
# src/env.ts and not in deploy-be.yml: the running app never reads it, only the
# test harness does. Unset → the integration tests skip. The harness migrates
# and writes to whatever this points at, so never point it at a real database.
# e.g. postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5433/reservetoday-test
TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# ---- Error monitoring (optional) ---------------------------------------------
# Set to your Sentry project DSN to turn on backend error reporting. Leave blank
# to disable — the app runs identically without it (see src/instrument.ts).
SENTRY_DSN=
